- **Current Standpoint**: The correct answer is **B. QT**.
- **Core Reasoning**: In an all-in preflop showdown against pocket Aces, suited holdings share identical flush and two-pair combinatorics. However, because straights are bounded at the Ace, every straight containing a Jack also requires a Ten, whereas a Ten can complete an additional low straight ($6\text{–}7\text{–}8\text{–}9\text{–}T$) and straight flush that a Jack cannot match. This asymmetry gives QT slightly higher total showdown equity against pocket Aces than QJ, thereby relatively minimizing Hero's equity the most.
}

